From f4afed045a15012e81fc59e5dd081dca0c62e608 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "nonggia.liang" <nonggia@192.168.0.101>
Date: Fri, 19 Jun 2026 22:10:04 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/7] feat(catalog-lakehouse-cust): implement first-write
 materialization
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

PR 3 of the cust catalog implementation. Implements spec §5.2.1: single-thread
first-write materialization for an unmaterialized mirror table.

New class CustTableOperations replaces CustReadOnlyTableOperations in the
loadTable path for unmaterialized tables. Unlike the read-only variant, it
supports commit by delegating to the underlying JdbcCatalog's TableOperations.

Key design — the "virtual-from" trick (spec §8.8):
Iceberg's BaseMetastoreTableOperations.commit rejects a non-null base when the
table row does not exist in JDBC. So CustTableOperations.commit ignores the
base argument from Iceberg's commit machinery and forwards null to localOps.
This makes JdbcCatalog take its create-table (INSERT) path and atomically
create the iceberg_tables row via the JDBC UNIQUE constraint (CAS).

This works because the client's newMetadata was built on top of the rewritten
upstream metadata (via AppendFiles or similar), so the upstream snapshot chain
is already incorporated — the local JDBC row becomes the first committed
state of the materialized table.

Changes:
- CustTableOperations: new TableOperations that holds (localOps, upstreamBase,
  upstreamIo). current()/refresh() return upstreamBase; commit delegates to
  localOps.commit(null, newMetadata); metadataFileLocation/locationProvider
  delegate to localOps so new metadata files land in catalog-a's warehouse.
- CustIcebergCatalog.loadUpstreamAndRewrite: now constructs CustTableOperations
  (commit-capable) instead of CustReadOnlyTableOperations, passing both
  super.newTableOps(ident) and the rewritten upstream metadata.
- TestCustIcebergCatalog: updated assertion to expect CustTableOperations.
- TestCustCommit: new tests for end-to-end commit path.

Tests:
- testFirstMaterialize: loadTable → commit → assert table materialized in
  local JDBC, reload returns the committed metadata.
- testMaterializedThenUpdate: after first materialization, reload takes the
  standard JdbcCatalog path; second commit succeeds and upstream is never
  consulted.
- 34 cust-module tests pass (was 32 in PR 2).

Out of scope for PR 3: concurrent CAS (PR 4), DDL rules (PR 5), production
UpstreamLookup wiring (PR 7), observability events (PR 7).

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
